# Compositor Thread Priority & Dual Keyframes

Interface fluidity hinges on a non-negotiable invariant: animations must execute on the GPU Compositor Thread, completely detached from main-thread Layout Thrashing.

## Why Animation Properties Must Never Cause Reflow

The browser rendering pipeline comprises three sequential stages:
1. **Layout**: Recomputes geometry, box dimensions, and fluid flow (extremely expensive).
2. **Paint**: Rasterizes colors, font glyphs, and box shadows.
3. **Composite**: Assembles pre-rasterized layers directly on the GPU (ultra-fast, zero main-thread block).

If an animation mutates `width`, `height`, `margin`, or `top/left`, the engine forces full-page recalculations on every frame, plummeting framerates below 60Hz.

## The Four Safe GPU Compositor Properties

TuffEx strictly confines all animations, loops, and micro-interactions to **four compositor properties**:

- **`translate`** (independent position translation, decoupled from transform matrices)
- **`scale`** (hardware scaling)
- **`rotate`** (hardware rotation)
- **`opacity`** (alpha compositing)

These properties live on dedicated GPU layers. Even during intense JavaScript execution, compositor animations remain locked at an unwavering 120Hz.

## Why CSS Variables are Banned Inside @keyframes

Developers often attempt parameterizing keyframes with CSS variables:
```scss
// Anti-pattern: Keyframes referencing var()
@keyframes bad-scale {
  0% { transform: scale(var(--base-scale)); }
  100% { transform: scale(1); }
}
```
**Fatal flaw**: Browsers cannot pre-compile keyframes holding dynamic variables on the compositor thread; they fall back to the main thread to resolve values on every tick, destroying hardware acceleration.

## Alternating Dual-Keyframe Replay Trick

When an effect must replay on demand (such as shaking an invalid input field):
The naive hack calls `void el.offsetWidth` to force-restart CSS transitions, paying a punishing synchronous layout penalty.

TuffEx employs dual-keyframe name alternation:

:::TuffCodeBlock{lang="scss"}
---
code: |
  // Two keyframes with identical bodies but distinct names
  @keyframes tx-pulse-a { 30% { scale: 1.1; } }
  @keyframes tx-pulse-b { 30% { scale: 1.1; } }

  .tx-box.is-pulse-a { animation: tx-pulse-a 240ms ease-out; }
  .tx-box.is-pulse-b { animation: tx-pulse-b 240ms ease-out; }
---
:::

Triggering a replay simply toggles between `is-pulse-a` and `is-pulse-b`. Changing the animation name restarts the animation with **zero DOM reflow penalty**.
